= Discussion
To reiterate, the main objective of the experimentation implemented in the methodology of this paper was to investigate a possible method into enhancing the structural effectiveness of wood through the combined usage of an alkaline sulfite-assisted delignification process and thermo-mechanical compressive process. As mentioned before, previous research has shown a high degree of correlation between the density of wood and its mechanical strength @niklas_2010_worldwide. Since density cannot be calculated directly and the only expected change in the dimensions of the wood was in the form of thickness after the compression, this is where the analysis began.

@thickness demonstrates the mean change in thickness with respect to pressure over the five segments obtained from each block. The initial expectation was for there to be a strong negative linear correlation between the thickness and the applied pressure for the sample. However, the values shown do not appear to have any strong positive or negative trend, besides the much higher thickness of the 3 MPa block. The most likely explanations for this come in the relative proportion of air each block contained and the springback effect. Blocks that were originally more porous and air-filled can be expected to compress more readily and fully than blocks containing less air within them or having that air sealed away in deep pockets. However, springback can also contribute to this difference in thickness. While the hot pressing technique somewhat mitigated the effects of springback, it cannot be completely eliminated. At very high pressures, internal stresses will cause cellulose fibers to behave elastically and somewhat rebound after compression. Thus, for the very high pressures (ie. the #qty(6, "MPa") and #qty(7, "MPa") trials), that elastic recovery may have overcome the plastically deforming practice of hot pressing.

@mass shows the mass of each whole block after pressing (the mean of five repeat weighings); the masses of the individual segments, which are used in the density calculations, are listed in @raw-mass. No cutting scheme is perfect, and to adjust for that, each block's width was measured to be able to effectively calculate volumes despite possible imperfections in cutting methods. These values are seen in @width.

@load-strength shows the mean maximum load wood segments of a specific thickness were able to bear before splitting. There is a notable decrease in value in the 5 and #qty(6, "MPa") trials, yet the #qty(7, "MPa") trial shows much better results that are on par with the lower-pressure trials. Since load strength is not a normalized quantity, this could be due to the dimensions of the block being used, or some defect/abnormality during chemical treatment or during the compression. This seems most likely in the face of the #qty(7, "MPa") block having values as high as the 3 and #qty(4, "MPa") blocks.

As each wood segment was compressed with greater and greater load, the segments began to deform. The movement of the midpoint under this load is known as the deflection, and was plotted against the load to create @load-def. This graph looks in accordance with standard graphs of this type: namely, there is a beginning "toe" section where values seem to be irrelevant; a linear section of elastic deformation; and a very high "plateau" at which plastic deformation and eventually failure occurs. Zooming in on the linear section in @s_load-def, the flexural stiffness can be calculated from this section as:

$ k = (d F)/(d delta) $

where $k$ is flexural stiffness, $F$ is load, and $delta$ is displacement. This gives a stiffness value of #q(stiffness, 3, "kN/mm").

Due to problems with the variable lignin setup, only three usable blocks (dissolved for 5, 6, and 8 hours) were obtained that could be used for testing. These blocks were divided into 3 flexural testing segments and 4 impact testing segments. Due to the very low quantity of data obtained, @impact presents the Charpy impact data in the form of box plots. For the 5 and 6 hour blocks, the mean and median impact energy show remarkable similarity, implying that there were not really any outliers in the data obtained and the overall skewness is likely low as well. The mean impact energy of the pine that was used in this paper is around #qty(4, "J"). Thus, the 6 hour blocks showed little to no improvement in impact energy, and the 5 hour blocks showed a small enough improvement that could be ascribed to random chance. The 8 hour blocks, however, absorbed only #q(stat("impact_j", 8), 2, "J") on average, far less than untreated pine, suggesting that such extended dissolution removes enough lignin and hemicellulose to severely embrittle the wood. The difficulty of analyzing these results is augmented by the extremely limited sample size. Thus, it was determined that moderate (5 to 6 hour) lignin dissolution had a negligible effect on the impact energy, while 8 hours of dissolution reduced it drastically. The impact energy is correlated to the ductility of a material. Wood is naturally very brittle, giving it a very low impact energy. Often, however, a material's strength and stiffness (which determines how brittle it is) are inversely correlated, but the methodology of this paper seemed to have little impact on the stiffness while showing great improvements in flexural strength. Thus, with a moderate dissolution time, the densification process retains wood's brittleness without worsening it, while augmenting the strength, which is what was desired for the results.

The information of @thickness, @mass, and @width culminate in @density, showing the mean density of the segments based on the pressure applied. Interestingly, the density peaked at the #qty(6, "MPa") block, even though that block had the lowest load strength in @load-strength. This seems contrary to the information provided in previous research that mechanical strength is proportional to density for wood. The density of the 3 MPa compression wood block was far below the other specimens, being even lower than the standard density of pine (#qty(0.49, "g/cm^3")). The combination of lignin removal and the evaporation of water during the hot pressing process represent the most likely culprits for the loss of mass that all blocks experienced; combined with the greater thickness this block ended with, the density of this block in particular would have dropped considerably. Since the thickness of the #qty(6, "MPa") block was lowest, it makes sense that this block also had the highest density. The drop-off at the #qty(7, "MPa") block was likely due to the same reasons as the increase in thickness in @thickness: namely, internal stress brought about by such extreme pressures allow elastic cellulose fibers to dominate the material after compression, surpassing the plastic deformation that was induced. Based on this information, it was concluded that the most optimal pressure for maximizing density likely resided around the #qty(6, "MPa") mark.

The information provided in @mor, @mean-mor, and @mean-mor-no-outlier describe the modulus of rupture for the various trials. The modulus of rupture is best explained as the load strength adjusted for the dimensions of the material, leading to much more comparable values for the MOR than there were for the load strength. Here, @mean-mor seems to show a positive trend but with an abnormally high value at the #qty(4, "MPa") mark. This can be explained by looking back at @mor, where the Trial 2 modulus of rupture value was incredibly high, beyond any other test by far. Thus, @mean-mor-no-outlier shows the mean moduli of rupture with that outlier removed, and the positive trend became much clearer. Nevertheless, all three of these figures demonstrated a clear increase in MOR as the pressure was increased, implying that even greater compressive loads may have been able to yield even better results.

In a similar manner to the modulus of rupture in @mor, @mean-mor, and @mean-mor-no-outlier, the strength to weight ratio (also called specific strength) figures (@stw, @mean-stw, and @mean-stw-no-outlier) show improvements in that ratio as the pressure is increased. The outlier of the #qty(4, "MPa") trials is even more evident in @stw, and completely obfuscates the results in @mean-stw. Thus, @mean-stw-no-outlier provides the best metric for comparing the various pressures and their effectiveness, which shows an impressive leap in strength to weight ratio. The #qty(7, "MPa") treatment seemed to be the most successful, being the strongest by weight at the end.

@stress-strain depicts a short section of linear increase, as would be expected from a stable stress-strain curve. The discrepancies at more extreme values are expected and not problematic: plastic deformation, parallax error, and mechanical backlash dominate these regions. The slope of this linear region can be more clearly seen in @short, with a value of #q(flexural-modulus, 0, "MPa"). Because $E=(d sigma)/(d epsilon)$, where $E$ is flexural modulus, $sigma$ is stress, and $epsilon$ is strain, flexural modulus can be estimated as #q(flexural-modulus, 0, "MPa"). Due to a variety of error factors including flexing of the press frame and parallax, this can only be safely considered as an estimate of flexural modulus.

Natural wood, the control value, was found to have an average modulus of rupture of #qty(natural-mor, "MPa"). This is close to literature values for pine, which typically range from #qtyrange(40, 80, "MPa"). Due to the minimal difference between the experimental and literature values for the control value, it is safe to assume that the experimental strength testing setup, although of course not as precise as a lab-grade equivalent, produces values suitable for at least comparison. This confirms that the treated wood has substantially higher flexural strength than natural wood, and also suggests that the procedure used here creates wood with similar strength to industry standard densified wood.

Each segment after testing showed essentially the same breaking pattern, as seen in @post-test. This pattern involved a break at what was originally the bottom (the side facing the camera in @post-test), and the crack then propagating up the segment until reaching the point of contact, snapping any fibers in its path. As the cellulose fibers on the bottom were the most stretched, it is reasonable that the first fibers to break were around that location. This shows a fundamental limitation in the usage of wood as a building material: once the load exceeds a certain value, wood has a tendency to snap and crush. Metals have a little give, and are able to yield instead of break. But wood, being far more brittle, cannot do the same. Nevertheless, it is key that the high strength to weight ratio that makes wood such an important building material was further amplified in the results of this paper, as was intended.


== Analysis of Error
Wood is a biological material, made of many very complex processes, all of which could substantially affect results. Most notable among these are biological variability, instrument precision, processing inconsistencies, and environmental factors.

The natural variability of biological materials was the most significant source of error in this study. Wood is anisotropic and natural. The blocks were cut from a standard pine plank, but due to the ring-like structure of the trees from which the wood comes, the density and ring structure can vary drastically over even just a few inches. @raw-block-mass, containing the block mass data, most accurately shows this: the mass of the #qty(3, "MPa") test block was #q(stat("block_mass_g", 3), 2, "g"), while the mass of the #qty(6, "MPa") test block was #q(stat("block_mass_g", 6), 2, "g"), or about #fixed((stat("block_mass_g", 6) / stat("block_mass_g", 3) - 1) * 100, 0)% more massive than the other block. For a block of roughly the same starting size to have a higher mass implies an inherently denser block or that it contained more moisture to begin with. This makes absolute density measurements rather arbitrary and difficult, while normalizing the data against the initial mass of each block would provide a clearer picture of the relative levels of densification.

Instrumental errors were likely to have also been a contributing factor, as the hydraulic press used a pressure gauge, limiting the precision to that of the analog measurement. Maintaining a constant pressure for extended periods of time was exceedingly difficult due to a loss of pressure over time in the manual hydraulic press. This occurred due to valve creep, and thermal contraction. The cutting process introduced further errors in precision, as the 5 equal-width pieces likely had some variation in geometry, directly affecting the calculation of the modulus of rupture, where small errors in measuring the dimensions can amplify in the final result.

The usage of citric acid for neutralization could have also led to error. The wood’s final density is partially triggered by the removal of moisture from the wood, which may have been prematurely initiated by the citric acid bath. The citric acid likely reacted with the sodium hydroxide to form anhydrous compounds capable of drawing water from the wood.

Environmental factors likely had an effect as well. All the samples were processed in a cold outside environment. While insulation was used, temperature fluctuations affect the properties of wood, which acts generally as a viscoelastic fluid. Wood is also hygroscopic, so if the samples absorbed moisture from the air, the cell walls would swell, reducing the strength and increasing the dimensions, while also triggering springback.